<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00SF">Remark <span data-tag="00SF">2.5.9.3</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a differential graded category and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\circ }$ denote its underlying category (in the sense of Construction <a data-tag="00PD" href="/tag/00PD">2.5.2.4</a>). Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\circ }$ is isomorphic to the underlying ordinary category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\Delta }_{0}$ of the simplicial category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\Delta }$ (in the sense of Example <a data-tag="00JU" href="/tag/00JU">2.4.1.4</a>). Both of these categories can be described concretely as follows: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\circ } \simeq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\Delta }_0$ are the objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the morphisms from $X$ to $Y$ in the category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\circ } \simeq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\Delta }_0$ are given by $0$-cycles in the chain complex $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)_{\ast }$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
